/-!
# Perfect ring complexes are generated by the ring

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0ATI, lemma-perfect-ring-classical-generator;
Derived Categories, Tag 05RB and definition-triangulated-subcategory
(blueprint: lem-sf-perfect-ring-classical-generator).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u w

open CategoryTheory

namespace DerivedCategory

/-- Perfect objects of the derived category of a commutative ring form precisely the
thick subcategory generated by the ring in degree zero. The singleton seed is nonempty,
so its `triangEnvelope` contains zero, is closed under all integer shifts and distinguished
cones, and is stable under retracts and hence under isomorphisms. Its universal property
identifies it with the intersection of all thick subcategories containing the ring.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0ATI, lemma-perfect-ring-classical-generator
(blueprint: lem-sf-perfect-ring-classical-generator).

Proof plan:
1. Let P be the property `IsPerfectRingComplex`. In a distinguished triangle, perfect
first and third terms imply a perfect middle term by lem-sf-perfect-ring-triangle-closure.
2. By the rotations of def-weil-triangulated-category and shift closure from step 3,
the other two two-out-of-three implications follow from step 1.
3. The zero complex is bounded finite-projective. Composing representing isomorphisms
preserves perfectness. Integer shifts reindex bounded finite-projective models; finite
sums of such models remain bounded finite-projective. Thus P contains zero and is closed
under isomorphisms, shifts and finite sums, by def-pc-perfect-ring-complex and
def-weil-abstract-derived-category.
4. By lem-sf-perfect-ring-summand, both summands of a perfect direct sum are perfect.
This is saturation; a retract splits as a direct sum in D(A), so P is thick.
5. The single-term complex A[0] is finite free, hence perfect by
def-pc-perfect-ring-complex.
6. For any thick T containing A[0], induct on the length of a bounded finite-projective
model supported in [a,b]. Finite sums belong to T by the split distinguished triangle
of lem-sbc-biproduct-triangle-distinguished. If a=b, the term is a summand of a finite
free module, so its shift belongs to T by sums, shifts and retract closure
(pur9-def-thick). The zero model also belongs to T.
7. For b>a, the degreewise short exact sequence from the last term in degree b to the
model and then its stupid truncation at b-1 gives the distinguished triangle of
lem-weil-pbc-complex-ses-triangle. The last term lies in T by the base case and the
truncation by induction. Triangle closure puts the model in T; isomorphism closure
puts every represented perfect object in T.
8. By pur9-def-thick, the thick closure is the intersection of all thick subcategories
containing A[0]. Steps 1-5 put that closure inside P; steps 6-7 put P inside every
such subcategory. Thus the two object properties agree. -/
theorem isPerfectRingComplex_eq_triangEnvelope_singleton (A : Type u) [CommRing A]
    [HasDerivedCategory.{w} (ModuleCat.{u} A)] :
    (IsPerfectRingComplex (R := A) : ObjectProperty (DerivedCategory (ModuleCat.{u} A))) =
      (ObjectProperty.singleton
        ((singleFunctor (ModuleCat.{u} A) 0).obj (ModuleCat.of A A))).triangEnvelope := by
  sorry

end DerivedCategory
